INT. COURTHOUSE ELEVATOR - DAY

Brass. Mirrored walls. A floor indicator that blinks between three and four.

CORDELIA FAIRCHILD-NWOSU, 40s, defense attorney, navy suit, files hugged to her chest, rides in silence. Beside her, TOBIAS REINHOLT, 30s, her client, tie knotted wrong, sweats through his shirt. In the corner, bailiff DESHAWN KOROMA, 50s, stands like a statue, arms crossed.

The elevator shudders. Stops. The lights dip.

TOBIAS
Is it supposed to do that?

CORDELIA
Yes.

DESHAWN
No.

Cordelia glances at him. He shrugs. A buzz crackles from the speaker panel.

DISPATCHER YBARRA (V.O.)
Elevator two, status?

DESHAWN
(into panel)
Stuck. Third and fourth.

YBARRA (V.O.)
Technician's twenty minutes out.

CORDELIA
Twenty? I've got a verdict at ten.

YBARRA (V.O.)
Ma'am, I don't control the building.

Cordelia faces the mirror. Tobias watches her in reflection.

TOBIAS
Miss Fairchild. Before we go in.

CORDELIA
Not now.

TOBIAS
I lied to you.

She does not turn.

CORDELIA
I know.

TOBIAS
You know?

CORDELIA
I read the file. I read the lab report you didn't mention. I've known since Tuesday.

Deshawn stares at the ceiling with great concentration.

TOBIAS
Then why did you stay?

CORDELIA
Because everyone deserves a defense. Even you.

The lights flicker. The elevator drops six inches and catches. Tobias grabs the rail. Cordelia finally turns and puts a hand on his.

CORDELIA (CONT'D)
When the doors open, you say nothing. Not one word.

YBARRA (V.O.)
Elevator two, repairs arriving.

The doors groan apart. Light from the hallway. Cordelia steps out first.
